+    A resting column has ``rho = rho(y)`` everywhere, and the wall particles are
+    part of it. Giving them the lattice mass ``rho0 dx^2`` instead puts a
+    ``rho0 (1 - rho(y_w)/rho0)`` density step at the wall face, which the
+    summation reads as a deficit on the *first fluid row*: measured -24 % on the
+    bottom row of a 0.35 m pool at ``dx = 20 mm``, i.e. a 18 m/s^2 spurious
+    downward acceleration (docs/verification_lake_at_rest.md, table 3).
+    """
+    if wall.ndim != 2:
+        raise ValueError("wall must be (W, D)")
+    return hydrostatic_density(wall[:, 1], cfg,
+                               surface=cfg.equilibrium_level
+                               ) * (lattice_mass_unit(cfg) / cfg.rho0)
+
